## Playback Seam Note

- Player startup now has a dedicated playback descriptor seam: `GET /api/library/movies/{id}/playback`.
- The current backend response is still direct-play oriented and points to `/api/library/movies/{id}/stream`.
- Frontend player code should consume the playback descriptor first, then treat `/stream` as an implementation detail.
- Curated now also has two expansion paths on that seam: an optional **browser-side native-player handoff** path used by the current frontend when explicitly configured, plus the backend session path `POST /api/library/movies/{id}/playback-session` and `/api/playback/sessions/{id}/hls/...` for HLS stream push. Native-player handoff is not assumed to exist and is disabled by default when settings do not provide it. The older `POST /api/library/movies/{id}/native-play` backend-launch route still exists as a legacy/native-shell hook, but the player page no longer depends on it for the default local-player action.
- Playback descriptors now carry structured decision diagnostics in addition to `mode`: `sessionKind`, `reasonCode`, `reasonMessage`, `sourceContainer`, `sourceVideoCodec`, `sourceAudioCodec`.
- Playback planning is no longer extension-only. Backend resolution now probes media metadata first, then chooses between `direct-file`, `remux-hls`, and `transcode-hls`.
- HLS session startup is now remux-first when the source is already HLS-friendly (`h264` video + compatible audio), with automatic fallback to hardware/software transcode profiles if stream-copy startup fails.
- HLS session governance now includes an idle-session janitor plus diagnostics routes: `GET /api/playback/sessions/recent` and `GET /api/playback/sessions/{id}`. The manager keeps active sessions and a bounded in-memory archive of recently closed or expired sessions so those endpoints remain useful after cleanup.
- The current in-page HLS browser path loads the npm-bundled official `hls.js/light` build on demand when native HLS is unavailable. Curated currently serves a single local rendition and uses the light build's MSE/XHR/event/level APIs; subtitle, EME/DRM, alternate-audio, and CMCD controllers from the full build are not required. Packaged desktop builds also import Outfit from `@fontsource/outfit`, so the shell no longer depends on Google Fonts or a CDN HLS script at runtime.
- `GET/PATCH /api/settings` now includes playback runtime preferences such as `nativePlayerEnabled`, `nativePlayerCommand`, `streamPushEnabled`, `ffmpegCommand`, `preferNativePlayer`, `seekForwardStepSec`, and `seekBackwardStepSec`, update/autostart flags such as `autoDownloadUpdates` and `launchAtLogin`, plus `launchAtLoginSupported`.
- Browser protocol handoff templates are **per-machine frontend state** stored in `localStorage` under `curated-native-player-browser-template-v1`; settings UI treats this as a browser launch template rather than a backend executable path.

## 2026-04 Library Path Reveal

- Backend now exposes `POST /api/library/paths/{id}/reveal` so Settings can open a configured library root in the OS file manager by persisted library-path id.
- The reveal handler validates that the stored library path still exists on disk and is a directory before opening it.
- Removing a library path still only updates Curated database bindings; it does not delete the actual local directory on disk.

## 2026-07 Actor Detail Route

- Frontend route `actor-detail` lives at `/actors/:actorName` and renders `ActorDetailView.vue` / `ActorDetailPage.vue`.
- Actor library cards and actor links from movie details navigate to the dedicated actor detail page instead of using the generic `library?actor=...` flow.
- The first actor detail implementation reuses the existing `libraryService.movies` cache and filters movies by exact actor name; a dedicated backend actor-movie endpoint remains a future option rather than current behavior.

## 2026-05 Library Storage Presence

- Backend storage presence checks live in `backend/internal/storagehealth` and persist expected backing-volume identities through `library_path_storage_bindings` in SQLite.
- Windows is the primary platform for volume identity in this phase; macOS/Linux use the fallback path probe and are future adaptation targets.
- New endpoints are `GET /api/library/paths/storage-status`, `POST /api/library/paths/storage-status/check`, and `POST /api/library/paths/{id}/storage-binding/rebind`.
- Frontend Web API startup checks abnormal paths, pushes storage warnings into the new toast/notification center, blocks scan/import actions when storage is unavailable, and allows deliberate rebind from Settings -> Video storage.

## 2026-04 Network Stability Update

- Actor scraping now supports backend-owned avatar caching and same-origin delivery via `GET /api/library/actors/{name}/asset/avatar`.
- Actor DTOs expose local-avatar state (`avatarLocalUrl`, `avatarRemoteUrl`, `hasLocalAvatar`), and backend handlers rewrite `avatarUrl` to the same-origin path when a cached file is ready.
- Movie preview assets still prefer local cache, and now have a backend proxy fallback when `media_assets.source_url` exists but `local_path` is missing.
- `media_assets` now persists source-side diagnostics for downloads: `source_provider`, `referer_url`, `last_http_status`, `last_error`, `last_fetched_at`.
- Settings / runtime now have a higher-level scrape scheduler field `metadataMovieStrategy` in addition to legacy `metadataMovieScrapeMode`.
- Provider health responses and task records now include richer machine-readable failure information (`errorCategory`, cooldown and rolling latency state where available).

## 2026-04 Homepage Daily Recommendations

- New backend endpoints: `GET /api/homepage/recommendations` and `POST /api/homepage/recommendations/refresh`; refresh accepts optional `preserveHeroMovieIds` and `excludeRecommendationMovieIds` so callers can regenerate today's recommendation IDs while persisting the current hero slate and avoiding the current recommendation rail where inventory allows.
- Backend persistence: SQLite table `homepage_daily_recommendations` stores the same-day display snapshot with `heroMovieIds`, `recommendationMovieIds`, `generatedAt`, and `generationVersion`; SQLite table `homepage_recommendation_states` stores per-movie recommendation memory (`lastRecommendedAt`, `recommendCount`, `skipUntil`, `updatedAt`).
- Generation behavior: the backend reuses the persisted daily snapshot only when its `generationVersion` matches the current algorithm version; stale same-day snapshots are regenerated and upserted. Current generation reads per-movie recommendation state, skips movies still inside `skipUntil`, weights candidates by quality/freshness plus "days since last recommended", 14-day cooling recovery, and logarithmic `recommendCount` penalty, then samples without replacement. It still uses recent daily snapshots as an additional exclusion ladder (`14 -> 10 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1 -> 0`) when inventory allows, and still adds actor/studio diversity penalties while assembling the same-day slate.
- Frontend wiring: `HomeView.vue` still builds the homepage portal locally for recent items, continue watching, and taste radar, but `heroMovies` and homepage recommendations are overridden by the backend snapshot when available.
- Frontend rollover: `src/composables/use-homepage-daily-recommendations.ts` watches the current UTC day key and re-fetches automatically after a day change; if a later refresh fails, the previous snapshot is kept in memory.
- Dev verification: `SettingsPage.vue` renders `SettingsHomepageDevTools.vue` only in dev mode with Web API enabled, exposing a manual refresh button in Settings -> About that calls `POST /api/homepage/recommendations/refresh` without a preserve body. The homepage "Today's Recommendations" refresh button sends `preserveHeroMovieIds` plus `excludeRecommendationMovieIds` and therefore refreshes the rail without rotating the hero carousel.

## 2026-05 App Update Installer Download

- `GET /api/app-update/status` and `POST /api/app-update/check` now include optional `installerDownloadUrl` and `installerSha256` when the latest GitHub Release exposes a Windows `.exe` installer asset with a digest. The backend prefers assets whose name or URL ends in `.exe` and contain `setup` or `installer`, then falls back to any `.exe` asset.
- New update flow endpoints: `POST /api/app-update/download` downloads the installer to the backend update cache and verifies SHA256, `POST /api/app-update/install` launches the verified installer after explicit user action, and `DELETE /api/app-update/downloaded-installer` clears the cached installer metadata/file.
- SQLite caches installer download and artifact state in `app_update_status` (`installer_download_url`, `installer_sha256`, `artifact_status`, downloaded file metadata, signature/install attempt fields), so Settings -> About can show download/install readiness until the cache expires or a manual check refreshes it.
- Settings -> About uses `installerDownloadUrl` as the primary update action and falls back to `releaseUrl` when no installer asset is available. The sidebar update badge still links to Settings -> About.

## 2026-05 Movie Import

- Frontend now exposes a top-bar `MovieImportDialog` next to the theme switch. It supports drag/drop, multi-file selection, and browser folder selection via `webkitdirectory`; selected videos are uploaded through the service layer and unsupported/duplicate files are skipped before submit.
- Settings -> Video storage now has a `defaultImportLibraryPathId` selector. The value is returned by `GET /api/settings`, updated through `PATCH /api/settings`, persisted in `config/library-config.cfg`, and used as the target for movie imports.
- Backend endpoint `POST /api/import/movies` accepts multipart form data with repeated `relativePath` + `files` fields and optional `totalBytes`. It copies accepted video files into the configured default library root, does not delete source files, does not overwrite conflicts, and returns a task of type `import.movies`.
- Large browser uploads use resumable session endpoints under `/api/import/movies/uploads`: `POST` creates a session and `.curated-import/<uploadId>` staging files under the target library root, `PUT /{uploadId}/files/{fileId}/chunks/{chunkIndex}` writes raw binary chunks by offset, `GET /{uploadId}` reports status, `POST /{uploadId}/commit` commits without overwriting existing library files before starting a scan, and `DELETE /{uploadId}` aborts the session.
- Migration `0029_movie_import_upload_sessions.sql` persists upload sessions, ordered files, synchronized chunk ranges, commit markers, expiry/diagnostics, and cleanup audits in `movie_import_upload_sessions`, `movie_import_upload_files`, `movie_import_upload_chunks`, and `movie_import_upload_cleanup_audits`. Chunk bytes are `Sync`/`Close`d before their range is recorded transactionally; counters are re-derived from the range ledger during startup recovery instead of trusting preallocated file sizes.
- Backend startup restores `uploading` and `committing` sessions with their original `import.movies` task IDs. Interrupted commits reconcile staging/final files without overwriting conflicts; an offline target reports `recoveryStatus: unavailable` and remains recoverable, while unsafe inconsistencies are marked `unrecoverable` with diagnostics.
- Active upload TTL is a sliding 24 hours. The 15-minute janitor writes cleanup audits before removing terminal/expired staging or old strictly named `.curated-import/upload_<16 lowercase hex>` orphans, defers when target storage is offline, rejects symlink/out-of-scope paths, and never deletes committed destination files.
- Import task metadata includes `targetLibraryPathId`, `targetPath`, `stage`, `totalFiles`, `completedFiles`, `failedFiles`, `copiedBytes`, `totalBytes`, `currentFileName`, `errorItems`, and optional `scanTaskId` / `scanError`. `ScanProgressDock` renders import-specific progress and `use-scan-task-tracker` shows success, partial-failure, and failure toasts before reloading movies.
- Import error codes include `IMPORT_SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE`, `IMPORT_TARGET_NOT_CONFIGURED`, `IMPORT_TARGET_UNAVAILABLE`, `IMPORT_NOT_ENOUGH_SPACE`, `IMPORT_CONFLICT`, `IMPORT_COPY_FAILED`, `IMPORT_CANCELLED`, `IMPORT_SCAN_FAILED`, `IMPORT_UPLOAD_PERSIST_FAILED`, `IMPORT_UPLOAD_UNRECOVERABLE`, and `IMPORT_UPLOAD_EXPIRED`.

## 2026-05 PIN App Lock

- PIN App Lock is the first privacy-lock slice for Curated, not a full multi-user permission system. It is disabled by default and is active only in Web API mode (`VITE_USE_WEB_API=true`).
- Backend persistence lives in SQLite migrations `0024_pin_app_lock.sql`, `0025_pin_app_lock_length.sql`, and `0027_auth_session_public_ids.sql`: `app_security_settings` stores `pin_enabled`, `pin_hash`, `pin_salt`, `pin_kdf`, non-secret `pin_length`, `session_ttl_minutes`, legacy-fixed-true `lan_requires_pin`, and `lock_on_restart`; `auth_sessions` stores server-side unlock sessions plus a separate random `public_id` used for safe management without exposing bearer-token IDs.
- PIN values are never stored as plaintext. `backend/internal/storage/security_repository.go` hashes PINs with Argon2id plus per-PIN salt and stores only the PIN length so `/lock` can render the correct number of cells.
- Auth endpoints are `GET /api/auth/status`, `POST /api/auth/setup-pin`, `POST /api/auth/unlock`, `POST /api/auth/change-pin`, `POST /api/auth/lock`, and `PATCH /api/auth/settings`. Backend middleware protects other `/api/*` routes when PIN is enabled and returns HTTP `423` with `AUTH_LOCKED`; `change-pin` is protected and also verifies the current PIN. Invalid `setup-pin` / PIN verification attempts are counted by both source IP and hashed client key; five consecutive failures start exponential backoff (1s up to 5m), returning `429 AUTH_RATE_LIMITED`, `Retry-After`, and `details.retryAfterSeconds`; a successful setup/unlock resets both counters, and audit logs never include PIN values.
- Browser auth state uses an HTTP-only `curated_auth` cookie. `unlock` and `setup-pin` accept `trustedForever`; trusted-forever sessions have no `expires_at`, receive a long-lived cookie, and survive restart-lock cleanup until explicitly revoked through `GET/DELETE /api/auth/sessions` or `POST /api/auth/sessions/revoke-others`. These APIs expose only random `public_id`, IP/UA/time metadata and a current-session flag, never the cookie bearer token. Regular sessions use `session_ttl_minutes` as an idle timeout; valid protected API use and `/api/auth/status` refresh extend `last_seen_at` and `expires_at`. Curated now uses one global PIN policy for local and LAN clients; `lanRequiresPin` remains a fixed-true response compatibility field and is no longer writable.
- Frontend auth state lives in `src/services/auth-lock-service.ts`. `src/services/auth-idle-lock-service.ts` listens for user activity, refreshes regular sessions before idle expiry, and redirects to `/lock` when the backend reports locked. Route `/lock` renders `src/views/LockView.vue`; the router redirects locked Web API pages to `/lock?redirect=...`.
- The lock screen intentionally has no media/background image and no on-screen numeric keypad. It shows PIN cells and relies on keyboard/numeric input, with a "Trust this device forever" checkbox.
- Settings includes a Security section (`src/components/jav-library/settings/SettingsSecuritySection.vue`) wired through `src/lib/settings-nav.ts` and `SettingsPage.vue` for initial PIN setup, PIN change, idle-lock delay, restart lock, lock-now, and trusted-forever session management. The old writable LAN PIN switch is removed because local and LAN clients share the same global lock. The session list shows safe public references as current-device/IP/UA/last-seen context, uses shadcn-vue badges/skeleton/separators, and confirms single-device or revoke-others actions without exposing bearer tokens. PIN setup, PIN change, and revoke confirmation use titled shadcn-vue dialogs instead of inline secret inputs or unconfirmed destructive actions.

## 2026-05 Curated Desktop Client Marker

- Electron injects `X-Curated-Client: desktop-electron`, `X-Curated-Client-Version`, `X-Curated-OS`, and `X-Curated-OS-Version` on requests whose origin matches the backend base URL. This is implemented in `electron/main.ts` via `webRequest.onBeforeSendHeaders` and helper functions in `electron/desktop-shell.ts`.
- Backend connected-client tracking checks this marker before generic User-Agent parsing. Marked requests are reported as `browser: "Curated Desktop"` with the Electron app version as `browserVersion`, and the desktop OS headers override Chromium's legacy `Windows NT 10.0` User-Agent token so Windows 11 can be displayed correctly.
- For regular browsers, CORS only allows same-origin, loopback development origins, and exact main-config `corsAllowedOrigins`; arbitrary credentialed Origin reflection and wildcard responses are rejected with `403 COMMON_FORBIDDEN`. Request Host is also checked against loopback / explicit LAN policy to mitigate DNS rebinding. Allowed responses advertise `Accept-CH: Sec-CH-UA-Platform, Sec-CH-UA-Platform-Version`; when those headers are present, `backend/internal/clienttracker` maps Windows platform version 13+ to `Windows 11`. The UI suppresses ambiguous `Windows 10.0` legacy UA display.
- The client-tracker key includes the marker, so Curated Desktop and a normal Chrome tab from the same IP with the same Chromium User-Agent remain separate connected-client rows.
- CORS allows desktop marker, desktop OS, Client Hints, and resumable upload headers.
